= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A subset is <relatively compact> in $M$ when its <closure> in $M$ is <compact>.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A subset is <totally bounded> if for every $\varepsilon>0$ it has a finite cover by balls of radius $\varepsilon$. If its <closure> is <compact>, the cover by all such balls has a finite subcover, proving total boundedness.

Conversely, cover $S$ by finitely many balls of radius $2^{-r}$ at stage $r$. Starting with all indices, at least one ball contains infinitely many of the currently indexed terms. Retain those indices as $\sigma_r$. These sets are infinite and nested, and two terms indexed by $\sigma_r$ have distance less than $2^{1-r}$. Choose increasing $n_r\in\sigma_r$; then $(x_{n_r})$ is Cauchy, because all its terms after stage $r$ lie in that same ball. If $M$ is complete it converges. To cover sequences in the <closure> as well, approximate their $n$th terms by points of $S$ at distance less than $1/n$ and apply this argument. The corresponding subsequence in the <closure> has the same limit. <Sequential compactness> therefore proves **total boundedness implies relative compactness in a complete ambient space**.

The <Arzelà-Ascoli theorem> says a subset of $C[0,1]$ is <relatively compact> exactly when it is uniformly bounded and <equicontinuous>. Pointwise boundedness together with <equicontinuity> is an equivalent version here. In the specified derivative-bounded family, the mean-value estimate gives $|f(t)-f(s)|\leq|t-s|$, uniformly over the family, while $\|f\|_\infty\leq1$. Thus this family is <relatively compact>.

For $V$, the image of the <unit ball> has sup <norm> at most one and derivative $f$ of sup <norm> at most one; it lies in that family, so **$V$ is <compact>**.

For $W$, take $f_n=n\mathbf1_{[0,1/n]}$, each of $L^1$ <norm> one. Then $Wf_n(t)=\min(nt,1)$. Every subsequence converges pointwise to zero at $t=0$ and to one at every $t>0$. This limit is discontinuous, so no subsequence converges uniformly to a continuous function. Thus **$W$ is not <compact>**. The same integration formula has different compactness properties because the domain unit balls impose different controls.

= 2
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <sublinear functional> on a real vector space satisfies $p(x+y)\leq p(x)+p(y)$ and $p(tx)=tp(x)$ for every $t\geq0$. The dominated real <Hahn-Banach theorem> says that a <linear functional> on a subspace satisfying $f\leq p$ extends to a <linear functional> on the whole space still dominated by $p$.

The limit-superior functional is finite on bounded sequences, positively homogeneous, and subadditive, since for every tail $\sup(\alpha_n+\beta_n)\leq\sup\alpha_n+\sup\beta_n$. Passing to the tail limits proves sublinearity. On the subspace $c$ of convergent real sequences, the ordinary limit is linear and equals $p$. Hahn-Banach therefore extends it to $\varphi$ on $\ell^\infty$ with
$$
\boxed{\varphi(x)\leq\limsup_n\alpha_n.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The extension already agrees with the usual limit on $c$. Alternatively, applying the domination inequality to both $x$ and $-x$ gives
$$
\liminf_n\alpha_n\leq\varphi(x)\leq\limsup_n\alpha_n.
$$
When the sequence converges, both endpoints are its limit, proving **$\varphi(x)=\lim\alpha_n$**. These bounds also give $\|\varphi\|=1$ and positivity on nonnegative sequences, but do not by themselves assert shift invariance.

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the infimum defining $q$, choose $k=1,j_1=1$. A finite shift leaves the <limit superior> unchanged, so
$$
\boxed{q(x)\leq\limsup_n\alpha_{n+1}=\limsup_n\alpha_n.}
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $x-Sx$, choose the consecutive shifts $j_r=r$, $1\leq r\leq k$. The average telescopes:
$$
\frac1k\sum_{r=1}^k(\alpha_{n+r}-\alpha_{n+r+1})=\frac{\alpha_{n+1}-\alpha_{n+k+1}}k.
$$
Its absolute value is at most $2\|x\|_\infty/k$. Taking the <limit superior> and then the infimum over $k$ proves **$q(x-Sx)\leq0$**.

For the requested functional, use the allowed sublinearity of $q$. On convergent sequences, every finite shifted average has the same limiting value, so $q$ equals the ordinary limit there. Hahn-Banach extends this limit to a linear $\psi$ satisfying $\psi\leq q\leq p$. Apply domination to $x-Sx$ and to $(-x)-S(-x)$ to obtain respectively $\psi(x-Sx)\leq0$ and $-\psi(x-Sx)\leq0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\psi(x)=\psi(Sx),\qquad\psi(x)\leq\limsup_n\alpha_n.}
$$
This is a <Banach limit>; the two-sided limit bounds also show positivity, <norm> one, and agreement with ordinary limits.

= 3
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <weak-star topology> on $X^*$ is the coarsest topology making every evaluation $\phi\mapsto\phi(x)$ continuous. Finite intersections of evaluation inequalities form a neighborhood basis. Distinct functionals differ on some $x$, so disjoint disks around their evaluation values give disjoint neighborhoods; thus the topology is Hausdorff. The <Banach-Alaoglu theorem> states that the closed <unit ball> of $X^*$ is <compact> in this topology.

If $f\in X^{**}$ is weak-star continuous, some $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ and $\varepsilon>0$ make $|f(\phi)|<1$ whenever $|\phi(x_i)|<\varepsilon$ for every $i$. If all evaluations vanish, every scalar multiple of $\phi$ lies in this neighborhood, forcing $f(\phi)=0$. Thus $f$ vanishes on the <kernel> of the map $E(\phi)=(\phi(x_1),\ldots,\phi(x_n))$. It factors linearly through the finite-dimensional range of $E$. Extending that factor algebraically to $\mathbb K^n$ gives scalars $a_i$ with
$$
f(\phi)=\sum_i a_i\phi(x_i)=\phi\left(\sum_i a_ix_i\right).
$$
Consequently **$f=J_Xx$** for $x=\sum_i a_ix_i$.

For the two induced topologies, let $B$ be the common <norm>-closed <unit ball> and write $\tau_j=\sigma(Y,X_j)$. Under either topology $B$ is <compact> by Banach-Alaoglu, and the ambient space is Hausdorff. If the two topologies are comparable, the identity map from $B$ with the finer topology to $B$ with the coarser one is a <continuous bijection> from a <compact space> to a <Hausdorff space>. Such a map is a <homeomorphism>, since it carries closed <compact> sets to closed sets. Thus **in the comparable case the topologies agree on $B$**, giving alternative (a).

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If the two ambient topologies are not comparable, neither is contained in the other. By the definition of inclusion of topologies, there is a $\tau_1$-open set absent from $\tau_2$, and a $\tau_2$-open set absent from $\tau_1$. These are the requested $U_1,U_2$, giving alternative (b). Together with the comparable case, this proves that at least one of the two alternatives always holds; it does not assume that the alternatives are mutually exclusive.

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take
$$
\boxed{\psi_1(\alpha)=\alpha_1.}
$$
It is evaluation against the first coordinate vector of $c_0$, hence continuous for $\sigma(\ell^1,c_0)$. The preceding continuous-dual result says that a functional continuous for $\sigma(\ell^1,c)$ must have coefficient sequence $(\gamma_\infty,\gamma_1,\gamma_2,\ldots)$ for a convergent sequence $\gamma$. To represent $\alpha_1$, it would require $\gamma_\infty=1$ but every $\gamma_j=0$, impossible.

Directly, $e_1-e_n\to0$ in $\sigma(\ell^1,c)$ because its pairing is $\gamma_\infty-\gamma_{n-1}\to0$, whereas $\psi_1(e_1-e_n)=1$ for $n\geq2$.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take
$$
\boxed{\psi_2(\alpha)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\alpha_n.}
$$
The series defines a <bounded linear functional> of <norm> one. It corresponds under $\Phi_2$ to the constant sequence $\gamma_j=1$, so is $\sigma(\ell^1,c)$-continuous. Its coefficient sequence is constantly one, which is not in $c_0$, so the continuous-dual result makes it noncontinuous for $\sigma(\ell^1,c_0)$. Directly $e_n\to0$ in that topology but $\psi_2(e_n)=1$.

= 4
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <open mapping theorem> says that a bounded <surjective> <linear map> $T:E\to F$ between Banach spaces is open. Here is a proof using only Baire and completeness. Surjectivity gives $F=\bigcup_{n\geq1}T(n\overline B_E)$, hence also a cover by their closures. Baire makes one such <closure> contain a ball $B(y_0,r)$. Taking differences gives $B(0,r)\subseteq\overline{T(2n\overline B_E)}$. Scaling yields some $\delta>0$ with
$$
B(0,\delta)\subseteq\overline{T(\overline B_E)}.
$$
If $\|y\|<\delta/2$, recursively use scaled versions of this inclusion to choose $x_k$ with $\|x_k\|\leq2^{-k}$ and residual $\|y-T\sum_{j=1}^kx_j\|<\delta2^{-k-1}$. The sum converges in $E$ to an element of <norm> at most one, whose image is $y$. Thus $B(0,\delta/2)\subseteq T(\overline B_E)$, and scaling gives $B(0,\delta/4)\subseteq T(\tfrac12\overline B_E)\subseteq T(B_E)$. Translating and scaling proves openness on every open set. This proof does not use the closed graph or inverse mapping theorem.

Apply it to the evaluation map $E:X\to\ell^\infty$, $Ef=(f(z_n))_n$. It is bounded with <norm> at most one and onto by hypothesis. Hence its image of the <unit ball> contains a ball about zero, yielding a constant $c$ and, for every $\alpha$, an interpolant $g$ with
$$
\boxed{g(z_n)=\alpha_n,\qquad\|g\|_\infty\leq c\|\alpha\|_\infty.}
$$
For completeness, scale a ball of radius $\eta$ inside $E(B_X)$ by $2\|\alpha\|_\infty/\eta$ to obtain $c=2/\eta$; zero data use $g=0$.

For each $n$, interpolate the data which are one at index $n$ and zero elsewhere, obtaining $g_n$ of <norm> at most $c$. The supplied factorization property gives $g_n=k_nh_n$ with $\|k_n\|_\infty=\|g_n\|_\infty\leq c$. Evaluation at $z_n$ gives $1=|k_n(z_n)h_n(z_n)|\leq c|h_n(z_n)|$, so
$$
\boxed{\inf_n|h_n(z_n)|\geq1/c>0.}
$$
The three enumerated properties of $h_n$ are supplied hypotheses for this argument, rather than additional requests to prove them.

= B
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}

= 5
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For the real lattice version of the <Stone-Weierstrass theorem>, let $f\in C_{\mathbb R}(\Omega)$ and $\varepsilon>0$. For any $x,y\in\Omega$, separation of points and constants give $h_{xy}\in L$ matching $f$ at both $x$ and $y$: take an affine combination of a separating function, and for $x=y$ take a constant. Fix $x$. The open sets where $h_{xy}>f-\varepsilon$ cover $\Omega$ as $y$ varies. Compactness gives finitely many such functions; their maximum $g_x$ belongs to the lattice $L$, satisfies $g_x>f-\varepsilon$ everywhere, and has $g_x(x)=f(x)$. The open sets where $g_x<f+\varepsilon$ now cover $\Omega$ as $x$ varies. Choose finitely many and take their minimum $h\in L$. Then $f-\varepsilon<h<f+\varepsilon$ everywhere. Thus **$L$ is uniformly dense** in the real continuous functions.

For the complex *-algebra, its real subalgebra $A_{\mathbb R}$ of <Hermitian C-star elements> contains the real and imaginary parts of every member. It separates points, because at least one of those parts distinguishes any pair distinguished by a complex function. Its uniform <closure> is a unital real subalgebra and is a lattice by the allowed result. The real theorem makes this <closure> all of $C_{\mathbb R}(\Omega)$. Approximating the real and imaginary parts separately proves **$A$ is uniformly dense in $C(\Omega)$**.

On $[0,1]$, the algebra of polynomials in $t^2$ is unital, closed under conjugation, and separates points because $t\mapsto t^2$ is injective there. Choose $p(t^2)$ within $\varepsilon/3$ of $a(t)$, and correct its endpoints by
$$
b(t)=p(t^2)+[a(0)-p(0)](1-t^2)+[a(1)-p(1)]t^2.
$$
This is still a <polynomial> in $t^2$ and has exactly the requested endpoint values. The correction has absolute value at most $\varepsilon/3$, because it is a <convex combination> of two errors of that size. Therefore
$$
\boxed{b(0)=a(0),\quad b(1)=a(1),\quad\|a-b\|_\infty<2\varepsilon/3<\varepsilon.}
$$

= 6
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

The <spectrum of an element> is $\sigma_A(a)=\{\lambda\in\mathbb C:a-\lambda1_A\text{ is not invertible in }A\}$. A <normal element of a C-star algebra> satisfies $a^*a=aa^*$.

For a <Hermitian C-star element> $h$, the <C-star identity> gives $\|h^2\|=\|h\|^2$. For normal $a$, commutation gives
$$
\|a^2\|^2=\|(a^*)^2a^2\|=\|(a^*a)^2\|=\|a^*a\|^2=\|a\|^4.
$$
All powers remain normal, so induction yields $\|a^{2^k}\|=\|a\|^{2^k}$. Taking the spectral-radius limit along these powers gives
$$
\boxed{\nu_A(a)=\|a\|\quad\text{for normal }a.}
$$
Let $\varphi:A\to B$ be a <C-star homomorphism>, without assuming continuity or preservation of the ambient unit. Put $p=\varphi(1_A)$, a <projection in a C-star algebra>. If $p=0$, then $\varphi=0$. Otherwise its image lies in the unital corner $pBp$ and it is unital as a map into that corner. An inverse in $A$ maps to an inverse in the corner, so $\sigma_{pBp}(\varphi(b))\subseteq\sigma_A(b)$. Apply this to the normal element $b=a^*a$ and use the <C-star identity> in both algebras:
$$
\|\varphi(a)\|^2=\|\varphi(a^*a)\|=\nu_{pBp}(\varphi(a^*a))\leq\nu_A(a^*a)=\|a^*a\|=\|a\|^2.
$$
Thus **every <C-star homomorphism> is <norm> non-increasing**, including nonunital maps.

= 7
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

A <unitary element of a C-star algebra> satisfies $u^*u=uu^*=1$. A <projection in a C-star algebra> satisfies $p=p^*=p^2$.

If $q=u^*u$ is a <projection in a C-star algebra>, the <C-star identity> gives $\|u(1-q)\|^2=\|(1-q)q(1-q)\|=0$. Hence $u=uq=uu^*u$. Conversely $uu^*u=u$ implies $(u^*u)^2=u^*u$, and $u^*u$ is a <Hermitian C-star element>, so it is a <projection in a C-star algebra>. Taking adjoints of the triple-product equality gives the corresponding equality for $u^*$, proving
$$
\boxed{u\text{ partial isometry}\iff uu^*u=u\iff u^*\text{ partial isometry}.}
$$
For nonzero $u$, let $p=uu^*$ and $q=u^*u$. These are projections, $u=pu=uq$, and $\|u\|=1$. The rectangular corner $pAq$ is closed because it is the range of the bounded idempotent map $a\mapsto paq$.

Define $\Theta:pAq\to qAq$ by $\Theta(a)=u^*a$. It lands in the corner, and its inverse is $b\mapsto ub$. The identities $uu^*a=pa=a$ and $u^*ub=qb=b$ verify both compositions. Both maps are contractive because $\|u\|=\|u^*\|=1$, so $\Theta$ is an <isometry>. For the proposed multiplication and <involution>,
$$
\Theta(au^*b)=\Theta(a)\Theta(b),\qquad\Theta(ua^*u)=q a^*u=a^*u=\Theta(a)^*.
$$
Thus the operations correspond exactly to the ordinary operations in the closed <C-star algebra> $qAq$. This proves associativity, <closure>, all <involution> identities and the C*-<norm> identity at once. Moreover $\Theta(u)=q$, so **the unit of $A_u$ is $u$**. If $u$ is unitary, $p=q=1$, and the map $a\mapsto u^*a$ is an onto isometric *-isomorphism from $A_u$ to $A$.

= 8
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

First suppose $x$ is a <positive linear functional>, without assuming boundedness. Every <Hermitian C-star element> $h$ is a difference of positive elements, since $\|h\|1\pm h\geq0$. Thus $x(h)$ is real. For a <Hermitian C-star element> $h$ of <norm> at most one, $-1\leq h\leq1$ gives $|x(h)|\leq x(1)$.

For arbitrary $a$, choose a scalar $\zeta$ of modulus one so that $\zeta x(a)=|x(a)|$. The <real part> of $\zeta a$ is a <Hermitian C-star element> and has <norm> at most $\|a\|$. Since $x$ is real on <Hermitian C-star elements>, it respects real parts, giving
$$
|x(a)|=x(\operatorname{Re}(\zeta a))\leq x(1)\|a\|.
$$
This proves boundedness and $\|x\|\leq x(1)$. Evaluation at the unit gives the reverse inequality, so **positivity implies $\|x\|=x(1)$**.

Conversely let $x$ be bounded with $x(1)=\|x\|=c\geq0$. If $c=0$, $x=0$ is positive. Otherwise normalize to $c=1$. For a <Hermitian C-star element> $h$ and real $t$, the <C-star identity> gives
$$
|1+itx(h)|\leq\|1+ith\|\leq\sqrt{1+t^2\|h\|^2}.
$$
Writing $x(h)=a+ib$ and squaring yields $-2tb+t^2(a^2+b^2)\leq t^2\|h\|^2$. Taking $t\to0$ through positive and negative values forces $b=0$. Hence $x$ is real on <Hermitian C-star elements>. If $0\leq a\leq1$, then $1-a$ has <norm> at most one, so $x(1-a)\leq1$ and $x(a)\geq0$. Scaling proves nonnegativity on every positive element, in particular every $b^*b$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{x\text{ positive}\iff\|x\|=x(1_A).}
$$
